    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>ce</strong> (masculine), <strong>cette</strong> (feminine) and <strong>ces</strong> (plural) go before the noun and match it: <em>ce livre, cette maison, ces livres, ces maisons</em>. They mean “this” or “that”.",
        "tag": "ce, cette, ces"
      },
      {
        "html": "A masculine noun that starts with a <strong>vowel or a mute h</strong> takes <strong>cet</strong>: <em>cet homme, cet appartement, cet hôtel</em>. A feminine noun stays <em>cette</em>: <em>cette école, cette histoire</em>.",
        "tag": "cet"
      },
      {
        "html": "To say “this one here” or “that one there”, add <strong>-ci</strong> (here) or <strong>-là</strong> (there) to the noun: <em>Ce pain-ci ou ce pain-là ?</em> In everyday speech, <em>-là</em> alone is common: <em>Je prends ce pain-là.</em>",
        "tag": "-ci, -là"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>quel, quelle, quels, quelles</strong> mean “which” or “what” and match the noun: <em>Quel livre ? Quelle heure est-il ? Quels jours ? Quelles langues ?</em> All four sound the same, except before a vowel in the plural (<em>quels amis</em>).",
        "tag": "quel…?"
      },
      {
        "html": "Without a noun, point with <strong>c'est</strong> or <strong>ça</strong>: <em>Qu'est-ce que c'est ? C'est ma clé. Ça coûte combien ?</em>",
        "tag": "c'est, ça"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>ce femme</em> → ✓ <em>cette femme</em>. ✗ <em>ce homme</em> → ✓ <em>cet homme</em>. ✗ <em>ces maison</em> → ✓ <em>ces maisons</em>. Next: <a href=\"/articles\">le, la, les, un, une</a> and <a href=\"/possessives\">mon, ma, mes</a>.",
